                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "**Extractability in AI search** is how easily an AI system can identify, understand, and reuse a specific passage from a webpage in its answer without needing surrounding context.[1][2]\n\nIt differs from **retrieval**:\n\n- **Retrieval:** The AI finds your page or content.\n- **Extractability:** The AI can cleanly lift a useful, accurate passage from it.\n- **Citation:** The AI ultimately uses and cites that passage.\n\nHighly extractable content typically:\n\n- Gives the answer immediately.\n- Uses clear, specific language.\n- States the subject explicitly instead of relying on pronouns like \u201cit\u201d or \u201cthey.\u201d\n- Makes each paragraph understandable on its own.\n- Uses descriptive headings, lists, tables, or FAQs.\n- Avoids burying key claims in long or ambiguous paragraphs.[3][4]\n\n**Example**\n\nLess extractable:\n\n> This approach has several benefits. It is also easier to implement and can improve performance.\n\nMore extractable:\n\n> **Extractability is how easily an AI search engine can lift a self-contained statement from a webpage and use it in an answer.**\n\nThe second version is more likely to be understood, reused, and cited because it defines the term directly and stands alone. Extractability is therefore a key part of optimizing content for AI search and generative-engine optimization (GEO).[2][5]",
